	•	Section 5 Comments/Quotes: The outcome was progression to circulatory death after withdrawal of care among potential DCD heart donors. In the primary external-validation cohort of 53 offers, the final multivariate logistic-regression model achieved AUC 0.88 (95% CI 0.78–0.97), sensitivity 1.00, specificity 0.50, PPV 64%, and NPV 100%. Accuracy and F1-score were not explicitly reported. Calibration was evaluated using intercept, slope, and Brier score; external validation showed an intercept of −1.55, slope of 1.51, and Brier score of 0.19, indicating systematic overprediction. Decision-curve analysis was not reported.

Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Consecutive offers were used, but only 53 of 110 offers assessed for primary external validation had all model inputs and were included. The authors acknowledge that “the exclusion of offers with incomplete data may have skewed the cohort composition,” creating substantial selection-bias risk in the primary validation analysis.
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: Predictors were measured before withdrawal of care and were available at the intended prediction time, which is appropriate. However, the authors describe “delimited, incomplete, and nonstandardized reporting of donor data in UNOS,” and retrospective clinical measurements and protocols were not standardized across donor hospitals.
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The outcome depended on whether circulatory death occurred before procurement was abandoned. Procurement was “generally abandoned 120 minutes after withdrawal of care,” but this period was occasionally shortened at the procurement team’s discretion, and elapsed time before abandonment was inconsistently documented; thus, outcome ascertainment was not standardized.
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Predictors were screened using univariate significance and backward elimination, several neurological predictors were potentially collinear, and the VIS coefficient was acknowledged to be unstable. Primary external validation used complete cases only, the external sample was small, and no internal optimism correction or hyperparameter-tuning strategy for the machine-learning comparators was reported, despite calibration and external validation being performed.
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The population, predictors, and outcome directly concern prediction modelling in potential DCD heart donors and are therefore directly applicable to AI and prediction-model research in thoracic organ transplantation.

